Date: Fri, 22 Jan 1993 15:00:31 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: PPL:  Someone born in a PPL

>> The legal theory is simple.  If a person is too immature to
>> understand his own interests (This person need not be a child.
>> Maturity is NOT age-dependent), a responsible adult, if acting as
>> that person's guardian, can legitimately make decisions for that
>> person (such as where to live).  At whatever age that person
>> becomes mature enough to grasp the consequences of his actions,
>> he then becomes an adult and can ignore his former guardian's
>> wishes.
 
>> Mark Read Pickens
 
> Yes, but who sets this legal theory?  Suppose we allow people
> to...sell themselves into slavery.  Can a parent then sell his child
> into slavery?
 
> Dale Worley
 
I am familiar with this legal theory and do find it simple, however, I
didn't mean to imply that someone who doesn't grasp it immediately (or just
doesn't agree) is either dumb or evil.  Of course, I'm not necessarily
ruling that out, either.  :-) 
 
I think a legal theory is discovered, not "set" by someone.  To me, the
details of how the theory is implemented is not the point.  If a child (or a
ward of any age) believes that he is/has been oppressed by his guardian, he
can sue for damages.  If, for example, you are on the jury, your job is to
determine which version you buy--the ward's or the guardian's.  If you
believe the guardian abused his position, you vote to award damages.  If you
believe the guardian actually acted in the ward's interests, you vote to
dismiss the suit.  (Would I hate to live in THAT house the next few days!)
 
Can people sell themselves into slavery?  Slaves, by definition, are
property.  Property has no right to own, let alone sell, anything.  At the
moment the prospective slave would have become property, he lost the right
to complete the transaction.  It seems to me we have a logical
contradiction.
 
Can people sell their children into slavery?  Parents call them "their"
children, but they don't actually own them; they own custody rights, not
title.  The right to use and the responsibility for the consequence of use
(or abandonment) are a package.  For example, it would not be legitimate to
transfer custody to a known child abuser.  It WOULD be legitimate to sell or
give custody, provided it's to the right person.  If, in contrast, a parent
sells custody to a known abuser, the child could later sue that parent for
negligence, as well as the actually abusive guardian; there would be shared
responsibility.
 
Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us
 
 

