> 
> The only people "chosen" to debate would be self-selected.  As I
> suggested in my posting, I can easily imagine Perry Metzger
> issuing a debate challenge to any number of newbees on the list.

You could imagine it, but you'd be wrong. I'm pretty uninterested in
debate. I do it well, but I don't have much energy.

When I started the extropians list (it seems like a long time ago but
it hasn't actually been that long), I wanted it to be a place where people
who generally believed the ideas already could discuss subtleties, exchange
useful information, provide each other with a group of like minded folks,
etc. I was hoping that it wouldn't turn into a place where basics got
debated over and over again, and I'm pretty much glad to see that although
my vision hasn't precisely worked out the way I wanted that the list does
fill the role I wanted -- a "safe haven" away from the madding crowd on
Usenet for extropians. Its become a sort of floating electronic coctail
party.

Debate is in general a fairly bad way to settle arguments. In my school
days, I was on the debate team, and I used to be able to win consistantly
on either side of an argument. I learned from this that persuasion by
argument, although a critical part of our society, is often not best served
when the proponents of an argument are professional persuaders, sometimes
unmatched in skill. Gentle, long term examination of the facts and study seems
to be a better way to educate people than watching debate, although debate
can prehaps be entertaining at times. 

I feel similarly about the Extropians list. I doubt that much would be served
by a formal debate process. I prefer the give and take, the ebb and flow of
ideas spilling through. I've learned quite a bit from people on the list,
and usually just in the course of casual and informal discussion here.

I think that the big problem with the list is that software isn't really
readily available to help people cope with the volume. However, the digest
form of the list may help some, and I may instigate some experiments soon
on a multi-threading version of the list that would let you automatically
subscribe and unsubscribe to topics.

In any case, as I often emphasize, things aren't too bad right now. All that
people need is a quick hand on the "delete" button and a sufficiently detached
attitude to keep them from taking anything too personally.

Perry

