From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Re:  EVOL: Stupidity and natural selection

> From: mickw@autodesk.com (Mick Washbrook)
> 
> >Only if you are a trader, and even then you are a fool if you believe it.
> 
> Perry, I have been reading a book recently on trading commodities. Apparently
> 60% of people who trade commodity futures end up losing money.

Well, you'd expect that half the trades made on the exchange must by
definition be money losing. After all, futures are a zero sum game, unlike,
say, the equities market. However, I'll point out that far more than 60%
of the people speculating lose money. I'd say the vast bulk do.
A buddy of mine at RefCo tells me that their mean time to blowout (what
you refer to a client who's lost all his money) is six months.

> Is this due to
> wanting to "wait a little longer" or is there an inherent trickiness in this
> area of speculation?

This is due to the fact that most people trading commodities futures don't
know what they are doing. Long ago, I realized that I don't know what I'm doing
in that regard and have stayed out of those markets.

> In your opinion, will computer modeling ever increase
> the accuracy of technical analysis in commodity futures? 

Technical analysis, for those that don't know, isn't what it sounds like.
Basically, its market voodoo. There are "patterns" technical analysis
people look for in the market, and they believe that the behavior of these
"patterns" will be a good indicator of what happens next in the market.
However, there is little concrete reason to believe that "technical
analysis" actually works. Efficient market theory isn't 100% accurate,
but its close enough to blow simple minded stuff like this out of the
water.

> Apart from "don't 
> try," what would be your advice to someone who's interested in allocating
> some disposable income to trying commodity speculation?

Commodities speculation is one of the easiest ways on earth to turn a large
pile of money into a small one. There are people out there who are really
really good at it, like Paul Tudor Jones, who I once had the priviledge
of working for. The bulk of us, however, are like tonedeaf musicians
trying to follow along with the band. There are legitimate ways to make
money in the markets, but they seem to involve extreme amounts of work and
a lot more than merely following past market patterns and hoping
against hope that they repeat themselves. A lot of the people in the markets,
by the way, use them purely for hedging and don't care if they lose money
in them because they are making a balancing amount of money in another
market. In any case, though, I'm reluctant to get into this sort of thing
myself. I wish anyone who tries their hand good luck. Who knows -- maybe you
are a natural and will make money at it. The odds are that you aren't, though.

Perry


