One of my favorite books was the rather poorly titled "Seven Habits of
Highly Effective People" (I'll look up the author if anyone is
interested. It was very helpful to me in thinking about long-range
priorities. There wasn't anything radically new...the author uses the
classic time-management quadrant

I   Urgent and Important- fire fighting, crisis
II  Important but not urgent-- all the things you want to do but
    don't get to...self improvement, investing time to save time
    later, etc
III Unimportant and Urgent -- phone calls, interruptions, requests
    to help with other people's work...easy and distracting
IV  Unimportant and not Urgent... time wasters, some e-mail, etc

Something about the presentation in the book was really helpful to me,
though. I think it was the emphasis on planning and values over
"quick-fix" techniques. I bought the tape for a reading-impaired
friend but I didn't like the tape.

